window.docContents[105].resolve({"/Slang/#Type-Systems--Slang___-Arithmetic-and-Boolean-Expressions--Evaluation-as-a-Relation":{"contents":"\n\ninductive Aexp.EvalR : Aexp → Nat → Prop where\n  | num (n : Nat) : EvalR (.num n) n\n  | plus {a₁ a₂ : Aexp} {n₁ n₂ : Nat} (h₁ : EvalR a₁ n₁) (h₂ : EvalR a₂ n₂) :\n      EvalR (.plus a₁ a₂) (n₁ + n₂)\n  | minus {a₁ a₂ : Aexp} {n₁ n₂ : Nat} (h₁ : EvalR a₁ n₁) (h₂ : EvalR a₂ n₂) :\n      EvalR (.minus a₁ a₂) (n₁ - n₂)\n  | mult {a₁ a₂ : Aexp} {n₁ n₂ : Nat} (h₁ : EvalR a₁ n₁) (h₂ : EvalR a₂ n₂) :\n      EvalR (.mult a₁ a₂) (n₁ * n₂)\n\n\nOne comment on the style of this definition.\nWe could instead have presented this relation with positional hypotheses --\nno names for the premises.\n\nnamespace ArithUnnamed\n\ninductive Aexp.EvalR : Aexp → Nat → Prop where\n  | num (n : Nat) : EvalR (.num n) n\n  | plus {a₁ a₂ : Aexp} {n₁ n₂ : Nat} : EvalR a₁ n₁ → EvalR a₂ n₂ → EvalR (.plus a₁ a₂) (n₁ + n₂)\n  | minus {a₁ a₂ : Aexp} {n₁ n₂ : Nat} : EvalR a₁ n₁ → EvalR a₂ n₂ → EvalR (.minus a₁ a₂) (n₁ - n₂)\n  | mult {a₁ a₂ : Aexp} {n₁ n₂ : Nat} : EvalR a₁ n₁ → EvalR a₂ n₂ → EvalR (.mult a₁ a₂) (n₁ * n₂)\n\nend ArithUnnamed\n\n\n\n\nIt will be convenient to have an infix notation for Aexp.EvalR. We'll\nwrite e ⇓ n to mean that arithmetic expression e evaluates to\nvalue n. The ⇓ symbol is typed \\Downarrow.\n\nnamespace Aexp\nscoped notation:55 e:56 \" ⇓ \" n:56 => EvalR e n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Type Systems\u0009Slang: Arithmetic and Boolean Expressions","header":"2.2. Evaluation as a Relation","id":"/Slang/#Type-Systems--Slang___-Arithmetic-and-Boolean-Expressions--Evaluation-as-a-Relation"},"/Sub/#Type-Systems--Sub___-Subtyping--Formal-Definitions":{"contents":"namespace StlcSub\n\nopen scoped MyGetElem\n\n\nMost of the definitions needed to formalize what we've discussed\nabove -- in particular, the syntax and operational semantics of\nthe language -- are identical to what we saw in the last chapter.\nWe just need to extend the typing relation with the subsumption\nrule and add a new inductive definition for the subtyping\nrelation.  Let's first do the identical bits.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Type Systems\u0009Sub: Subtyping","header":"8.2. Formal Definitions","id":"/Sub/#Type-Systems--Sub___-Subtyping--Formal-Definitions"}});